TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive suppression",
    "counter strategy",
    "recommended mix",
    "winning"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to operationally define a suppression-conditional recommended mix from play-type and defensive matchup data",
    "How to measure adherence to the recommended mix without lookahead bias",
    "How to assess predictive performance for series/game outcomes given the small number of series"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No previous project studies were retrieved, so no prior findings establish anything about this question."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No evidence from prior project work is available, so the feasibility, effect size, and best methodology are entirely unestablished."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question proposes a predictive relationship that can be tested with the available data if the recommended mix is clearly defined and the time-series structure is respected.",
    "The primary unit (54 series) is likely the number of playoff series, which is a very small sample for robust prediction when the predictor is a derived metric that itself requires estimation.",
    "Switching to a game-level analysis (using hundreds of games) with series-level clustering or hierarchical modelling would substantially increase power and is feasible given the per-game files.",
    "The construct 'suppression-conditional recommended mix' is under-specified; an operational definition must specify what constitutes a defensive suppression, what counts as a counter strategy, and how the recommended mix is computed (e.g., league-wide efficiency, matchup-specific optimisation).",
    "Adherence could be measured as divergence (e.g., Kullback-Leibler) between actual offensive play-type distribution and recommended distribution in possessions following a suppression."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Exactly what defensive events count as 'suppression' (e.g., forced isolation, denied pick-and-roll, forced long mid-range) and whether they can be reliably tagged from play-by-play or derived play-type data.",
    "Whether the recommended mix is static for a team or varies opponent-by-opponent, series-by-series, or in-game, and how to avoid overfitting when deriving it.",
    "Whether 'winning' refers to winning the series (binary) or winning individual games (binary/margin) and what level of analysis (series vs. game) the investigator intended."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so the decision is based solely on methodological assessment of the current data and question."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.35,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 54 series available; detecting a realistic predictive effect requires careful power analysis, and series-level analysis is likely underpowered."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The definition of suppression-conditional recommended mix is ambiguous and may be measured with substantial noise, diluting any true signal."
      },
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "If the recommended mix is derived using within-series data or future outcomes, the test will be trivially inflated; a strict temporal or leave-one-series-out scheme is required."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior project studies were retrieved; thus no answer from earlier work is available."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "54 series provide limited statistical power; a moderate association (e.g., AUC 0.65) would require a larger sample to reach conventional significance."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Operationalising 'suppression-conditional recommended mix' and 'adherence' likely involves many arbitrary choices (threshold, similarity metric, time window) that heavily influence the result."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Better teams may both adhere more to optimal strategies and win more; the observed association could be largely driven by team quality rather than adherence itself."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If the recommended mix is derived from the same playoff games used to test the prediction, the predictor becomes contaminated with outcome information."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Without pre-registration of a single definition, the many researcher degrees of freedom in defining the exposure can lead to inflated false-positive risk."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.1,
    "rejected": 0.1,
    "untestable": 0.4,
    "artifact": 0.2,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The predictive value of game-level adherence to a pre-defined suppression-conditional recommended offensive mix for game outcome (win/loss), controlling for team strength.",
    "unit": "One playoff game (team‑game observation).",
    "sample": "All playoff games from the 2022-23 through 2025-26 seasons for which play-by-play and defensive matchup data are available, with the 54 series used as cluster identifiers.",
    "power": "With ~800 playoff games, a logistic regression with team fixed effects and series random effects can detect an odds ratio of ~1.5 at 80% power if the exposure is continuous and moderately reliable. A simulation-based power analysis should confirm the minimum detectable effect.",
    "controls": "Pre-game team Elo rating or regular-season SRS to partial out general team quality; home/away indicator; playoff round; rest days.",
    "falsification": "Test whether adherence predicts win margin in regular-season games late in the season after playoff motivation is accounted for, or whether within-series adherence differs between wins and losses for the same team using a permutation test that breaks outcome dependence. An alternative falsification: correlate adherence with a future measure of team quality (e.g., next-season win total) to rule out that it merely proxies for underlying strength.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register one primary definition of suppression events (e.g., possessions where the defense forced the offense into a play type whose league-average points per possession was below 0.90 in the previous season) and one adherence metric (e.g., symmetric Kullback-Leibler divergence between observed play-type proportions and recommended proportions). All other specifications are exploratory.",
    "decision_rule": "Primary test: logistic regression of game win on adherence, controlling for pre-game Elo, with standard errors clustered by series. Significance at p < 0.05 (two-tailed). Direction: higher adherence predicts higher win probability.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API per-game files as of 2026-06-16, with play-by-play and derived play-type possession records from the project's graph database; 2025-26 playoffs data frozen on June 21, 2026."
  }
}

